The aggregate region is fat: for \(k\) comparable to \(T\), it becomes a region in two smooth variables with congruence \(w-v\equiv k^2\pmod{2k}\). Harper’s averaged AP theorem has enough level here; it does not require \(\log x/\log q\to\infty\). There is also a small-prime contribution of order \(n^{-2}\) to the normalized variance, so an uncentered second-moment estimate needs more care than the proposed transfer allows.
