@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+# Fixed-u QS moment calculation (scratch research note)
+
+Status: the coordinate identities, local calculations and elementary H2 lemma below are PROVED. The proposed full mean-square expansion in the last section is UNVERIFIED: its outlined truncation argument is not a substitute for a complete uniform estimate for the arithmetic main terms. This distinction is intentional.
+
+## 1. Exact coordinates
+
+Let B=2^n, I=[B/2,B) intersected with the integers, H=B/2, and take common parameters y,T for the dyadic block. Write f(s)=1_{P^+(s)<=y}. Common parameters matter: if T varies by a fixed factor across the dyadic block, the raw variance of Z contains that deterministic variation.
+
+Put x=m+t, v=x^2-N. The condition 1<=t<=h, with m=ceil(sqrt(N)), is exactly
+
+    2x-1 <= v <= 2x(h+1)-(h+1)^2-1.
+
+Indeed, for t fixed the possible v are consecutive integers from 2xt-t^2 to 2x(t+1)-(t+1)^2-1, and these intervals adjoin as t increases. Also impose N=x^2-v in I.
+
+Consequently
+
+    H E Z = sum_{x,v} f(v) 1_{x^2-v in I}
+                    1_{2x-1 <= v <= 2x(T+1)-(T+1)^2-1}.
+
+For k=t'-t>0 put w=v+2kx+k^2, h=T-k. Then
+
+    H E Z^2 = H E Z + 2 sum_{1<=k<T} sum_{(v,w) in R_k} f(v)f(w),
+
+where R_k is given by
+
+    w-v == k^2 (mod 2k),
+    x=(w-v-k^2)/(2k),
+    x^2-v in I,
+    2x-1 <= v <= 2x(T-k+1)-(T-k+1)^2-1.
+
+These identities include square N and all endpoint conventions.
+
+For fixed t,t', (m,r)->(v,w) has determinant 2(t-t'). Its image is thin. For k comparable with T, after summing t, the v,w region has two dimensions of order sqrt(B)T and lattice index 2k. It is fat. For k much smaller than T it has aspect ratio roughly k/T. The thin-fixed-t description does not justify calling the aggregate second moment an inaccessible binary correlation.
+
+## 2. First moment
+
+PROVED: for fixed 0<c<1/2, y=T=B^c (integer rounding understood), u=1+1/(2c),
+
+    E Z = T(rho(u)+O_c(1/log B)).
+
+A convenient proof exchanges x and v in the first identity. The multiplicity in x is a piecewise smooth weight, up to O(1) per v. Its total O(sqrt(B)T) counting error divided by H T is O(B^{-1/2}). The two x-edge bands have relative size O(T/sqrt(B)). Partial summation of the global Dickman estimate gives the assertion. Uniformly on compact u-ranges, the global estimate is Psi(z,y)=z rho(log z/log y)(1+O(1/log y)). No short-interval theorem is needed.
+
+With the de Bruijn-Saias expansion one also obtains the first-order term
+
+    E Z/T = rho(u) + rho'(u)/log y *
+      (gamma - 3/2 + (3/2)log 2) + O_c((log y)^-2)
+
+when u is in a compact subset of (3,infinity). The coefficient uses the density rho(v)+gamma rho'(v)/log y, the average log(t/T)=-1+o(1), and E log(N/B)=log 2-1+o(1).
+
+## 3. Applicable AP input
+
+Adam J. Harper, arXiv:1208.5992v1 (2012), Theorems 1 and 2. Set Psi=Psi(z,y), v=log z/log y, H(v)=exp(v/log^2(v+1)). There are absolute K,delta>0 such that for log^K z<=y<=z:
+
+* Theorem 1, Q<=sqrt(Psi): sum_{q<=Q} max_{(a,q)=1}|Psi(z,y;q,a)-Psi_q(z,y)/phi(q)| is at most
+
+      C_A Psi(H(v)^(-delta)(log z)^(-A)+y^(-delta))
+          + C sqrt(Psi) Q (log z)^(7/2).
+
+* Theorem 2, Q<=Psi: the corresponding sum of squares, summed over reduced a, is at most
+
+      C_A Psi^2(H(v)^(-delta)(log z)^(-A)+y^(-delta))
+          + C Psi Q.
+
+The arbitrary-A constants can be ineffective. Both have effective alternatives omitting (log z)^(-A). The condition log z/log q -> infinity belongs to Harper's other, individual-AP theorem, not to these averaged theorems.
+
+At z approximately sqrt(B)T and Q approximately T, Q/sqrt(Psi) is a negative power of B when c<1/2. Hence the ineffective versions give every fixed logarithmic saving. Finite logarithmic grids of endpoints and polynomial-logarithmic divisor truncations can be paid for.
+
+Nonreduced residues must be retained. Since v==w (mod k), one has gcd(v,k)=gcd(w,k)=g. Write k=gh and v=ga,w=gb; then (a,h)=(b,h)=1, and b-a==hk (mod 2h). For g of logarithmic size, f(ga)=f(a). The factor 2 requires separate parity/valuation bookkeeping. It cannot simply be discarded.
+
+Using these changes, logarithmic rectangle approximations to R_k, and gcd truncation, the leading-order AP replacement gives
+
+    E Z^2/T^2 = rho(u)^2+o_c(1),
+
+and therefore PROVED: Z/T -> rho(u) in probability over all integers in I. This gives H1 outside o(B) integers (for T=y), which alone does not transfer to almost all balanced semiprimes. A rate better than B/(log B)^2 requires the finer calculation below.
+
+## 4. A proved local lower bound
+
+Fix an odd prime ell. The first-moment calculation in N==a (mod ell), using smooth AP counts at this fixed modulus, gives
+
+    E[Z | N==a (mod ell)]/T - E Z/T
+      = -rho'(u) log(ell)/((ell-1)log y) * (a/ell)
+          + o_c,ell(1/log y).
+
+Here (0/ell)=0. To see the coefficient, smooth v divisible by ell have count Psi(z/ell,y); smooth v in the ell-1 nonzero classes have equal counts to the necessary precision. The number of x satisfying x^2==v+a (mod ell) is 1+(v+a/ell). The resulting correction is
+
+    ((a/ell)/(ell-1)) * (ell Psi(z/ell,y)-Psi(z,y)).
+
+Taylor expansion gives the displayed term. In particular the law of total variance proves
+
+    liminf (log y)^2 Var(Z)/T^2
+       >= rho'(u)^2 log(ell)^2/(ell(ell-1)) > 0.
+
+Thus Var(Z)/(E Z)^2=o(n^-2) is FALSE in this dyadic fixed-u setting. This lower bound does not require proving the full variance asymptotic.
+
+## 5. The candidate corrected second-moment expansion
+
+UNVERIFIED as a global assertion in this note. The following local algebra is PROVED, but the inference from Harper's theorem to the stated complete arithmetic main-term expansion needs a separate detailed proof.
+
+For a prime ell define the p-adic local function
+
+    A_ell(N)=log(ell)*( E_{x in Z_ell} v_ell(x^2-N)-1/(ell-1)).
+
+For odd ell and ell not dividing N, this is (N/ell)log(ell)/(ell-1). Higher valuations of N also affect it. Thus conditioning only on N modulo a squarefree primorial does not remove every fixed local contribution. One needs increasing prime-power precision, or explicit centering by the local functions.
+
+Let A_W denote the conditional projection of these functions onto N mod W, for W a finite product of prime powers. Conditional projection at ell^a includes the geometric tail above valuation a; simple truncation of the valuation sum is not exactly the same projection. Let
+
+    b(N)=log 2 + (1/2)log(N/B) + gamma-1,
+    C = log(2)^2/3
+          + sum_{ell odd prime} ell log(ell)^2/((ell-1)^2(ell+1)).
+
+The local functions have mean zero; their variances sum to C<infinity. For odd ell the variance is ell log(ell)^2/((ell-1)^2(ell+1)); for ell=2 it is log(2)^2/3. One proof expands v_ell(s)-1/(ell-1) in additive Fourier modes. At a primitive frequency mod ell^j its coefficient is 1/(ell^(j-1)(ell-1)). Averaging s=x^2-N multiplies this by the normalized quadratic Gauss sum. Parseval gives the stated values (the mod-2 Gauss sum vanishes; at 2^j,j>=2 its squared magnitude is 2^(j+1)).
+
+The proposed expansion is
+
+    lim_{B->infinity} (log y)^2 E[
+       Z/T-rho(u)-rho'(u)(b(N)-A_W(N))/log y ]^2
+      = rho'(u)^2*(C-Var(A_W)).                    (*)
+
+If (*) is established, it proves both
+
+    Var(Z)/T^2 ~ rho'(u)^2/(log y)^2 *
+       [C + (1-2(log 2)^2)/4]
+
+and
+
+    #{N in I: Z < rho(u)T/2} = o(B/(log B)^2).
+
+For the second implication fix W. The proposed center is uniformly rho(u)+O_W(1/log y), so Chebyshev bounds limsup (log y)^2 times the failure probability by a constant times C-Var(A_W). Then let W increase through products of increasing prime powers. This is an iterated-limit argument; no unjustified uniformity in an enormous primorial is needed.
+
+What must be proved for (*): after the fat-region AP replacement, expand the nonreduced-residue main terms through total order two, justify truncation of all gcd and inclusion-exclusion divisors at polynomial-logarithmic size with error o((log y)^-2), and match the resulting coefficients with the CRT local projections. The formal tail estimates involve convergent sums such as sum_g (log g)^j/g^2 and sum_{d,e}(gcd(d,e)/(d^2 e^2))(log(de))^j. These make (*) plausible and a concrete target; they do not on their own verify the entire uniform transfer, including the parity terms and the moving region.
+
+The input for high-precision global smooth counts is Hildebrand-Tenenbaum, JTNB 5 (1993), Theorem 1.8 and (1.29), followed by expansion of the de Bruijn-Saias approximant. Its exponentially small error is stronger than any logarithmic saving in a fixed-u compact range.
+
+No growing-u version is asserted. For y=L[1/2,a], the relative local perturbation has size approximately (log u)/log y and its square has order (log n)/n. Moreover discarding close pairs using f(v)f(w)<=f(v) now loses a factor 1/rho(u). These issues prevent simply carrying over the fixed-u outline.
+
+## 6. An elementary fruitful-pair lemma
+
+PROVED. Suppose x<z, x,z>sqrt(N), and (x^2-N)(z^2-N)=Y^2. Put X=xz. Then
+
+    (X-N)^2-Y^2=N(z-x)^2>0,
+
+so X-Y>N. If x,z<=ceil(sqrt(N))+T and T=o(sqrt(N)), then
+
+    X+Y <= 2(ceil(sqrt(N))+T)^2-N <2N.
+
+Thus N<X-Y<X+Y<2N. Their product is divisible by N, but neither is divisible by N. If N=pq, p!=q, this forces gcd(X-Y,N) to be proper. This also proves that trivial dependencies of length two cannot occur in the stated short interval. Rank/nullity does not guarantee a dependency of length two.

